  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "157d5043",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2.4 — Chronological split, embargo and the common anchor set\n",
    "\n",
    "**Split.** `get_splits()` (shared with notebook 1) returns four boundaries (all \"end\" values exclusive):\n",
    "\n",
    "`[ inner train ) [12 h embargo) [ validation ) [12 h embargo) [ test ]`\n",
    "\n",
    "* **inner train** `t < inner_train_end` — fits every candidate during model selection;\n",
    "* **validation** `val_start ≤ t < train_end` — scores the candidates; the same dates for every model family;\n",
    "* **outer train** `t < train_end` (= inner train + embargo + validation) — the final refit of the selected configuration;\n",
    "* **test** `t ≥ test_start` — scored once, for reporting only.\n",
    "\n",
    "The embargo is `EMBARGO_HOURS = 12`, the longest horizon: a row within 12 h of a boundary has a target on the other side of it, so it is dropped from the earlier period (not moved to the later one). This holds at **both** boundaries — inner train → validation and outer train → test.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Common anchor set (regular hourly clock, no interpolation).** `data.index` has holes where `dropna()` removed hours (three gaps in the active period: ~233 h, ~223 h and 22 h — far too long to interpolate credibly, so they are left as gaps). A row is used as a prediction anchor only if the **24 hours ending at it are all present and consecutive on the hourly clock**. This rule is applied to **every** model, including the single-timestep ones, so all architectures and all history lengths are trained and scored on exactly the same anchor hours. Windows of an anchor may reach back into an embargo period — those hours are *inputs* observed before the anchor, never targets."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "5faaf5a3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "note: fc_temp_prevday still missing 364h after the short-gap fill; bridging with an unlimited forward-fill of its own past values (flagged in fc_temp_prevday_stale)\n",
