   ],
   "source": [
    "def make_lstm(n_features, n_targets, lookback=MAX_LOOKBACK, units=64, dropout=0.0, lr=1e-3, optimizer=\"adam\", seed=0):\n",
    "    keras.utils.set_random_seed(seed)\n",
    "    model = keras.Sequential([\n",
    "        layers.Input(shape=(lookback, n_features)),\n",
    "        layers.LSTM(units, dropout=dropout),\n",
    "        layers.Dense(32, activation=\"tanh\"),\n",
    "        layers.Dense(n_targets),\n",
    "    ])\n",
    "    model.compile(optimizer=make_optimizer(optimizer, lr), loss=\"mse\")\n",
    "    return model\n",
    "\n",
    "NEURAL_BUILDERS[\"LSTM\"] = make_lstm\n",
    "run_grid(\"LSTM\", hist=True)\n",
    "run_grid(\"LSTM\", hist=False)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "70454078",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2.7f — Attention (compact Transformer-encoder block), history and t only\n",
    "\n",
    "A lightweight *attention-based* sequence model, loosely in the spirit of TFT but deliberately **not** a full TFT: dense projection → learned positional embedding → one 4-head self-attention layer → residual + layer norm → small feed-forward → residual + layer norm → global average pooling → dense head. With ~1k training anchors, TFT's variable-selection networks, gating and quantile loss would mostly add parameters to overfit with. With `lookback=1` (t only) the attention weight is 1 by construction and the block reduces to a residual feed-forward network — an expected degeneracy, not a bug."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
