   "source": [
    "VPD_LOW_KPA = 0.4    # PROVISIONAL -- validate with the agronomy expert\n",
    "VPD_HIGH_KPA = 1.6   # PROVISIONAL -- validate with the agronomy expert\n",
    "\n",
    "def _runs(flags):\n",
    "    \"\"\"(start, end) positions of maximal runs of True in a boolean array.\"\"\"\n",
    "    f = np.asarray(flags, dtype=bool)\n",
    "    if not f.any():\n",
    "        return []\n",
    "    d = np.diff(np.r_[0, f.astype(int), 0])\n",
    "    return list(zip(np.where(d == 1)[0], np.where(d == -1)[0] - 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "def event_scores(real, pred, kind, target_times):\n",
    "    real = np.asarray(real); pred = np.asarray(pred)\n",
    "    thr = VPD_LOW_KPA if kind == \"low\" else VPD_HIGH_KPA\n",
    "    y_true = (real < thr) if kind == \"low\" else (real > thr)\n",
    "    y_pred = (pred < thr) if kind == \"low\" else (pred > thr)\n",
    "    y_score = -pred if kind == \"low\" else pred\n",
    "    # target times must be contiguous hourly for runs to be episodes\n",
    "    assert (np.diff(target_times.values) == np.timedelta64(1, \"h\")).all(), \"episode logic needs contiguous hours\"\n",
    "    tn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix(y_true.astype(int), y_pred.astype(int), labels=[0, 1]).ravel()\n",
    "    n_ev = int(y_true.sum())\n",
    "    true_eps, alarm_eps = _runs(y_true), _runs(y_pred)\n",
    "    detected = sum(y_pred[s:e + 1].any() for s, e in true_eps)\n",
    "    onset = sum(bool(y_pred[s]) for s, e in true_eps)\n",
    "    false_eps = sum(not y_true[s:e + 1].any() for s, e in alarm_eps)\n",
    "    return {\n",
    "        \"n_hours\": n_ev,\n",
    "        \"Precision (h)\": precision_score(y_true, y_pred, zero_division=0),\n",
    "        \"Recall (h)\": recall_score(y_true, y_pred, zero_division=0),\n",
    "        \"F1 (h)\": f1_score(y_true, y_pred, zero_division=0),\n",
    "        \"False-alarm rate (h)\": fp / (fp + tn) if (fp + tn) else np.nan,\n",
    "        \"AUPRC (h)\": average_precision_score(y_true, y_score) if 0 < n_ev < len(y_true) else np.nan,\n",
    "        \"n_episodes\": len(true_eps),\n",
    "        \"episode recall\": detected / len(true_eps) if true_eps else np.nan,\n",
    "        \"onset hit rate\": onset / len(true_eps) if true_eps else np.nan,\n",
    "        \"alarm episodes\": len(alarm_eps),\n",
    "        \"false alarm episodes\": false_eps,\n",
    "        \"episode precision\": (len(alarm_eps) - false_eps) / len(alarm_eps) if alarm_eps else np.nan,\n",
    "    }\n",
    "\n",
    "def event_table(kind):\n",
    "    rows = {}\n",
    "    for src, vlist in vpd_sources.items():\n",
    "        for h in horizons:\n",
    "            tt = test.index + pd.Timedelta(hours=h)\n",
    "            per = [event_scores(vpd_real[h], v[h], kind, tt) for v in vlist]\n",
    "            rows[(src, h)] = pd.DataFrame(per).mean()\n",
    "    t = pd.DataFrame(rows).T\n",
    "    t.index.names = [\"forecast\", \"horizon (h)\"]\n",
    "    return t\n",
    "\n",
    "vpd_event_low, vpd_event_high = event_table(\"low\"), event_table(\"high\")\n",
    "print(f\"LOW VPD (< {VPD_LOW_KPA} kPa, provisional) -- model: {best_key[0]} ({best_key[1]})\")\n",
    "display(vpd_event_low.round(3))\n",
    "print(f\"HIGH VPD (> {VPD_HIGH_KPA} kPa, provisional)\")\n",
    "vpd_event_high.round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "32371db3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2.14 — Deployment information regimes: persistence / snapshot / historical / NWP / oracle\n",
    "\n",
    "The feature ablation (2.3–2.9) turns individual column groups on and off; this section compares explicit **tiers of information available at prediction time**, for every architecture:\n",
    "\n",
    "| regime | what's allowed |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **persistence / seasonal persistence** | nothing learned (2.6) |\n",
    "| **snapshot** | information *at* time *t* only: current interior climate + exterior weather + time + current actuators + cluster label — **no** history, **no** forecast; only the *t only* variants |\n",
    "| **historical** | snapshot **+** observations up to *t* (actuator rolling sums; the past window for the history variants) — the `+ cluster` rung of 2.7, reused |\n",
    "| **NWP (matched)** | historical **+** the `previous_day1` forecast at *t+h* for **temperature, humidity, wind and radiation** — trained fresh |\n",
    "| **oracle (matched)** | historical **+** the **observed** future exterior weather at *t+h* for the **same four quantities** — trained fresh; not deployable |\n",
    "| NWP (all 5 vars) | the `+ day-ahead forecast` rung of 2.7 (adds forecast precipitation), reused for reference |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why matched variables.** The earlier comparison put NWP with precipitation + global shortwave radiation against an oracle with PAR radiation and no precipitation, so part of the gap could come from *which* variables each side had, not from forecast error. Both matched regimes now use the same four quantities. One difference cannot be removed with the data available: radiation is **global shortwave (W m⁻²)** in the forecast and the **PAR band** at the station. The two are closely proportional (2.14a reports their correlation and the train-fitted slope), and every input is standardised, so this is a spectral-band/unit difference rather than a different driver — but it is stated explicitly rather than hidden. The forecast's own error against the station (below) gives context for the NWP–oracle gap.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What oracle is — and is not.** Oracle is a **reference that uses the observed future weather** at the station. It is *not* a guaranteed upper bound on performance: the station measures a point outside this greenhouse, the forecast is a smoothed grid-cell value that can be easier to exploit, and a model with oracle inputs is still fit on ~1k anchors — so an oracle model can come out *worse* than its NWP counterpart for some architectures, and the gap should be read with the CIs below.\n",
    "\n",
    "All regimes share the anchors, split, selection criterion, refit rule and seeds of 2.7."
   ]
  },
