   "source": [
    "## Summary & next steps\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this notebook now measures, and how.**\n",
    "\n",
    "* Interior **temperature + humidity** at **+1 h / +6 h / +12 h**, one multi-output regression, scoped to the period where all climatic systems operate.\n",
    "* **One protocol for every model** (2.4, 2.7): chronological *inner train → validation → test* split with a 12 h embargo at both boundaries; scalers and cluster labels fit on the training portion of each stage; one common set of anchor hours with a complete, gap-free 24 h history (gaps are discarded, not interpolated); selection by mean CVRMSE **in original units** on the **same validation dates** for tabular and neural models; the winner refit on the full outer train; neural models reported as the mean ± std over 5 seeds.\n",
    "* **Model choice on validation, reporting on test** (2.8b): the main model — and the one used for the ablations and the VPD analysis — is the validation-selected one.\n",
    "* **Two baselines** (2.6): persistence and seasonal (same-hour-yesterday) persistence. At +6/+12 h the second is the fair reference, and the real forecasting gain is measured against it (2.8b, 2.8d, 2.10).\n",
    "* **Uncertainty** (2.8d, 2.9, 2.14c): paired circular block-bootstrap 95 % CIs (24 h blocks) for the main differences — selected model vs baselines, history vs t only, neural vs tabular, each information block, and each regime step. With ~12 days of test data these intervals are wide; differences whose CI includes 0 should not be claimed.\n",
    "* **History length** (2.7, 2.7n): the history variants select `L ∈ {3, 6, 12, 24}` h on validation, and 2.7n reports the full sensitivity curve (1–24 h) on identical anchors — the direct answer to whether the last few hours carry most of the signal and whether 24 h adds noise.\n",
    "* **Information regimes** (2.14): snapshot / historical / NWP / oracle, with **NWP and oracle using matched weather variables** (temperature, humidity, wind, radiation; the shortwave-vs-PAR band difference is stated). Oracle is a reference using observed future weather, not a guaranteed upper bound.\n",
    "* **Climatic-system features** (2.9, 2.12): their importance share and ablation effect are printed from the run's numbers; permutation importance is computed separately for temperature (°C) and humidity (%RH).\n",
    "* **VPD events** (2.13b): hour-level and **episode-level** detection, for the model and both baselines, with thresholds explicitly marked as provisional pending the agronomy expert.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Run order.** Notebook 1 first (it writes `cluster` and `cluster_sel` and the split it used; 2.4 asserts that both notebooks agree), then this notebook top to bottom.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ideas to push further**\n",
    "* **Control / what-if model:** predict the *change* in temperature/humidity caused by an actuator action — there the actuators are the inputs being set, which is where the climatic-system data should pay off (notebook 3).\n",
    "* Collect more than one season: a longer test window would narrow the bootstrap intervals and cover more regimes.\n",
    "* A rolling-origin evaluation (several consecutive validation/test windows) would give a less noisy model choice than a single validation block.\n",
    "* Feed the control **rules** (`inside data/rules/`) as setpoint features."
   ]
  }
